findings=[
 {'id':'Q347','title':'Accepted tiny positive sampling temperatures turn tied maxima into a fixed last-token choice','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/infer/decoder/sample.rs:95,102,140;crates/intel/esp/src/infer/decoder/provider.rs:374','status':'reproduced exact unchanged sampler on finite tied logits, two top-p settings and128seeds; no model execution','evidence':'prepare_generation admits finite positive1e-40. Sampler forms f32 reciprocal infinity and zero-times-infinity NaN for each equal maximum. With logits[3,3], every one of128seeds returns token1, both with no top-p and top-p0.9; temperature0.01 controls choose both tokens. Equal logits should retain equal probability at any positive temperature. The first hypothesis that a unique maximum[2,3] would lose was disproved and is retained in the v1 failure receipt; this finding is only the verified tie bias.','diagnostics':['decoder_text_math_probe_v2_receipt.json','decoder_text_math_v1_failure_receipt.json'],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Avoid an overflowing reciprocal, for example divide the centered logit by the positive temperature, and keep a non-finite distribution policy explicit. This is a bounded mathematical fix candidate, not a model/GPU repair.'}},
 {'id':'Q348','title':'Expected-token rank reports first place for a NaN expected logit','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/infer/decoder/train.rs:459,484;crates/intel/esp/src/infer/decoder/provider.rs:325','status':'reproduced exact helper with finite control and non-finite rows; tally caller verified by complete source','evidence':'expected_token_rank counts logits strictly greater than logits[expected_id]. If that expected value is NaN, every comparison is false and rank is1, including an all-NaN row. ranking_tally calls the helper on next_token_logits without a finiteness check and increments passed for every positive cutoff. Both trainer forcing receipts use this same tally, so shared evaluation authority can certify invalid numerical output. Finite input weights are admitted carefully, but that does not validate output arithmetic. No real model overflow, trainer run or published invalid EvalReport was executed here.','diagnostics':['decoder_text_math_probe_v2_receipt.json'],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Refuse non-finite rows before evaluation and validate expected_id bounds. An invalid model evaluation must not be scored as a successful rank.'}},
 {'id':'Q349','title':'Streaming detokenization emits an incomplete Unicode replacement then permanently loses the completed text','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/infer/decoder/provider.rs:109,116,174;crates/intel/esp/tests/tinyllama_real.rs:87','status':'reproduced exact StreamObserver and decode method with real retained tokenizers0.20.4 ByteFallback+Fuse; no model/Burn execution','evidence':'Real tokenizer decoding byte tokens C3,A9 gives replacement character for the first token, é after the second and éx after an ASCII third token. StreamObserver immediately emits the replacement because emitted is empty. Every later decode fails starts_with(emitted), and finish also refuses to flush it, returning Ok with text replacement only while token_ids still decode to éx. A stop sequence é is also missed after the prefix mismatch. ASCII and cross-token ASCII stop controls pass. The comment promising that held mid-byte text arrives later is false for this admitted tokenizer. Existing real-tokenizer test compares generate and streaming, which use the same observer, on an ASCII Paris prompt. Checkpointed generation independently replaces final text with full decode at completion, so this receipt does not claim identical final corruption on that path.','diagnostics':['decoder_text_math_probe_v2_receipt.json'],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'document streaming owner repair and cross-route parity as a high-effect issue','reason':'Track stable decoder output before callback delivery, handle incomplete byte sequences explicitly and share stop/stream completion policy with checkpoint generation. Include split Unicode and stop cases; no hour-long model build is needed for this reproduction.'}},
 {'id':'Q350','title':'Controlled cancellation can return text that its callback never received','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/infer/provider.rs:242,251;crates/intel/esp/src/infer/stub.rs:120;crates/intel/esp/src/infer/actor.rs:235','status':'reproduced public API over two unchanged source modules; actor outcome handling verified by source','evidence':'The default generate_streaming_controlled suppresses on_token when cancelled becomes true, returning Break to generate_streaming. The underlying provider correctly includes the current fragment because Break means it was delivered, so controlled returns it as successful text anyway. Cancel on the first fragment yields alpha with zero callback deliveries. Cancel on the second yields alpha beta while callbacks contain only alpha. This violates the trait text-concatenation contract at the portable API boundary. The actor separately chooses Cancelled and discards the Ok result, so this is not evidence that the actor announces Finished after cancellation. SessionProvider also inherits this default rather than forwarding controlled calls into a decoder-specific implementation.','diagnostics':['infer_cancellation_probe_receipt.json'],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Make the controlled wrapper return the fragments actually forwarded or use explicit cancellation outcome semantics. Preserve ordinary callback Break including its current fragment.'}},
]
